      <item>
          <title>Trynix - Run any NixOS package in your browser</title>
          <pubDate>Tue, 24 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
          <author>Lzon</author>
          <link>https://lzon.ca/posts/curio/000039-trynix/</link>
          <guid isPermaLink="false">https://lzon.ca/posts/curio/39</guid>
          <category domain="https://lzon.ca/posts/curio/">Curios</category>
          <description xml:base="https://lzon.ca/posts/curio/000039-trynix/">&lt;p&gt;Link: &lt;a href=&quot;https://trynix.dev/&quot;&gt;https://trynix.dev/&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This is a fun project that allows you to run any NixOS package in your browser.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;span id=&quot;continue-reading&quot;&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This reminded me of &lt;a rel=&quot;noopener external&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot; href=&quot;https://www.thestrangeloop.com/2013/the-birth-and-death-of-javascript.html&quot;&gt;The Birth and Death of Javascript&lt;/a&gt;. It’s a ‘future-history’ style performance in which the speaker explains all software, even the OS itself, gets reimplemented as web apps running in a browser.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Using Trynix I tried to run Firefox within Firefox, and it worked! I recommend you find your favorite app in the package registry and give it a go.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Source: &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.thestrangeloop.com/2013/the-birth-and-death-of-javascript.html&quot;&gt;https://www.thestrangeloop.com/2013/the-birth-and-death-of-javascript.html&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
</description>
